WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.380
- So one of the hardest things

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:05.010
when you are starting as an entrepreneur,

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.110
when you're trying to start
some type of anything,

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:10.020
whether it's a business or a
nonprofit or an organization,

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.830
is you're going to wanna share your idea

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:14.530
and what you're working
on with a lot of folks,

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:16.360
and you're going to get
different reactions.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.000
And it's not unlikely that you're going

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.147
to get a lot of folks who
will tell you things like,

00:00:21.147 --> 00:00:22.557
"Well, this is already done before.

00:00:22.557 --> 00:00:24.230
"Why do you think you're
going to be able to do it?"

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:25.887
or "This has never been done before.

00:00:25.887 --> 00:00:28.080
"Why do you think you're
going to be able to do it?"

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.870
And it's very hard to judge whether,

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:35.430
okay, are they just being
naysayers and just being negative

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.010
and I should just power through it,

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:40.130
or should I listen seriously
to what they have to say?

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:43.020
And like all things, take
this all with a grain of salt,

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.820
it's hard to know for sure,
but I like to think about

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:50.400
whether people are coming
from a point of view

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.630
of that they really want you to succeed.

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:54.620
And if there are people in your life

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:56.770
that you know at the end of
the day have got your back

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:58.580
and really want you to succeed,

00:00:58.580 --> 00:00:59.890
I would listen to what they're saying.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:02.480
Now, it doesn't mean what they're
saying is absolutely true.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.400
It's completely possible
that they might have

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.030
a different tolerance
for risk than you do,

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:08.430
but it's worth processing.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:10.210
It's worth at least listening.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:11.867
And sometimes when people say,

00:01:11.867 --> 00:01:14.557
"Oh, well, this has been tried before.

00:01:14.557 --> 00:01:18.190
"Why do you think you
doing it is going to work?"

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:20.300
Well, the answer to that is sometimes

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:24.000
how you do it matters a lot
more than the idea itself.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.410
Khan Academy is an example of that.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:27.390
I wasn't the first person to think

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:30.950
that online could be a way
to disseminate information.

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:32.250
I wasn't the first person to think

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.330
that software could personalize
education for folks.

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:38.750
I wasn't the first person
to make content on YouTube

00:01:39.708 --> 00:01:43.170
or to help teach people through video.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.790
But there was something about
how it all came together

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:47.940
and also the time and space

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:52.290
and how accessible it was
that things caught on.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.660
And that would've been
very hard to predict

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.760
had I not at least tried.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.760
And the way that I protected myself

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.770
from both the positive and negative,

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:04.737
positive and negative feedback
(laughs) was I told myself,

00:02:04.737 --> 00:02:05.847
"You know what?

00:02:05.847 --> 00:02:07.237
"This is all good information.

00:02:07.237 --> 00:02:08.907
"I'm going to listen to
what people have to say,

00:02:08.907 --> 00:02:10.097
"but I'm also going to try it.

00:02:10.097 --> 00:02:13.097
"And I'm gonna try it in a
way that I can start to learn

00:02:13.097 --> 00:02:16.950
"what is actually working
and what's not working."

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:20.670
And if you can work on
your day job, so to speak,

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:22.440
while you start to
fiddle and you can start

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.040
to build those data points
and points of confirmation

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.310
that you might be on to something,

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:30.200
well, then it makes you a
little bit more resilient

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.740
to what folks might tell you.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:34.040
And you should keep listening,

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:36.220
but you shouldn't rest everything

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.853
on necessarily what everyone tells you.

